{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,26]],"date-time":"2025-10-26T13:53:39Z","timestamp":1761486819156,"version":"3.37.3"},"reference-count":20,"publisher":"IOP Publishing","issue":"11R","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[1999,11,1]],"date-time":"1999-11-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":941414400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/iopscience.iop.org\/page\/copyright"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[1999,11,1]],"date-time":"1999-11-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":941414400000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/iopscience.iop.org\/info\/page\/text-and-data-mining"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["iopscience.iop.org"],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Jpn. J. Appl. Phys."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1999,11,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p> \n  Energy-filtered reflection electron microscope (REM) images of well-defined surfaces were taken for the first time using an ultrahigh vacuum electron microscope equipped with an omega filter. Si(111)7\u00d77 and Si(111)5\u00d72-Au surfaces were imaged. Images using electrons with energy losses of 11.3 and 22.6 eV (single and double surface plasmon excitation) showed lattice fringes of Si(111)7\u00d77(2.3 nm in spacing) and Si(111) 5\u00d72-Au(1.7 nm). The observation gave the first experimental evidence that diffraction image contrast is conserved in images formed by electrons inelastically scattered by small angles in REM, as in the case of transmission electron microscopy. \n  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1143\/jjap.38.6540","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2002,10,1]],"date-time":"2002-10-01T23:04:55Z","timestamp":1033513495000},"page":"6540","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1088\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":6,"title":["Image Conservation in Inelastically Scattered Electrons  in Reflection Electron Microscopy"],"prefix":"10.35848","volume":"38","autho